content.thinking:
The consumer passes `includeImages: wantsImages || acceptsImages(model)` — so when the model accepts images, includeImages is always true (even with no hints). And `pages: hints.length ? hints : undefined` → default 1..20. So for a PDF with a vision model and no page hints: includeImages=true, pages undefined → my module extracts pages 1..20 with renders. The worker renders up to 20 pages → possibly heavy, and only the first two renders are kept (`MAX_IMAGES = 2` in both modules).

This is precisely the case my defensive cap would fix: request renders only for the pages that can possibly be kept (first 2 in the requested, in-range set), extract text-only for the rest.

Note: with `hints` (≤2 pages), the requested set is ≤2 → no waste. The wasteful case is "no hints, default 20 pages, vision model". That's a realistic case (user asks a question with a PDF attached, the model reads images).

Hmm wait, is it wasteful in output terms? The consumer `evidence` yields `images` for up to 2 pages. With no hints, the "kept" renders are pages 1 and 2. Then the writer gets images of pages 1–2 plus text of 1–20. So capping renders at 2 pages preserves output exactly.

There's one more consideration: text extraction in two calls means two worker spawns (probe + text + images = 3 calls). Currently: probe + (rest with images) = 2 calls. Hmm, but I can be smarter: the probe call [1] can carry the render for page 1 (page 1 is a render candidate if requested). Then:
- Call 1 (probe, images=includeImages): [1] → totalPages, render for page 1.
- Call 2: pages 2..N: if includeImages && wanted has ≥2 in-range pages → images for the second render candidate only... but the `includeImages` flag is global per call: it would render every page in call 2. Not good.

Options: split call 2 into two calls: [2] with images and [3..20] text-only → 3 calls total. Or accept rendering two pages in call 2: [2, 3..20] with images → renders 19 pages. No.

Hmm, alternatively, when includeImages && no hints: images for pages 1..2 only. I could structure the whole extraction as:
- renderPages = first min(2, ...) in-range requested pages → call with `includeImages: true`
- textOnlyPages = the remaining requested in-range pages → call with `includeImages: false`

And the probe (when totalPages unknown) can be merged into the render call if renderPages is non-empty (its first element is 1... not necessarily: requested could be [7]). Hmm, complexity.

Let me estimate the real cost/necessity. Is rendering 20 pages actually a problem? Each render: scale = min(2, 1600/max(w,h)) → for a letter page, 1600×2070 px RGBA = 13 MB buffer for `data` before PNG encoding (pdfium gives RGBA pixels), PNG-encoded. The worker keeps `record.image.data` (PNG bytes) for 20 pages, say 50–500 KB each → ~1–10 MB total, fine. The RGBA intermediate is per-render, freed after. So memory is OK-ish (128 MB limit refers to old generation; 13 MB RGBA is fine). Time: rendering 20 pages at 1600px might take several seconds (pdfium is fast; my test rendered 2 pages in ~1s total including startup). So ~5s for 20 pages. Hmm, within the 60s timeout, but it wastes turn latency.

Given the consumer explicitly asks for images on every call with a vision model, and `truncatedPages`/page text clamps to ≤20, I think the defensive cap is worth it — the spec's "dropping extra pages silently" is preserved (pages beyond the first two simply carry no render), while rendering work drops from 20 pages to 2.

Hmm, but hold on. Let me re-read the assignment once more: "When `includeImages` is true, write each returned PNG to `_discover/pdf/<sha48>/page-0001.png` (vault-relative path also added as `imagePath` on that page record) and keep base64 in `images`; cap at 2 images total and 8 MiB each, dropping extra pages silently."

"write each returned PNG" — each PNG returned by the worker. So the spec clearly expects the worker to return PNGs and the parent to write each of them... but then "cap at 2 images total" → only up to 2 written. So under the literal spec: the worker returns N, the parent writes at most 2. My implementation does exactly that (drop beyond cap silently). If I add the render cap, the worker returns ≤2 → same observable output. No spec violation.

Implementation approach: keep it simple and clear.

Restructure the "missing" computation:
- Determine `wanted` = requested ∩ 1..totalPages (only when totalPages known).
- `renderSet` = includeImages ? wanted.slice(0, MAX_IMAGES) : [] — actually, the render candidates should be the first two pages we will *keep* renders for = the first two in ascending requested order. ✓ (output loop iterates ascending and keeps the first two renders).
- For pages in `wanted`: reuse cached text unless (includeImages && renderSet.has(number)).
- Missing text pages → extract text-only.
- Missing render pages → extract with images. Can both sets be merged into one call? Only if includeImages pages are the only pages in that call. So:
  - if renderMissing.length: one call with `pages: renderMissing, includeImages: true` (text + renders).
  - textMissing (excluding renderMissing) → one call with `includeImages: false`.

Hmm, but this could produce up to 3-4 worker calls (probe + render call + text call). With the unknown-totalPages case: probe [1] with images? Page 1 may or may not be a render candidate. Simpler: probe always text-only ([1]); then compute render/text splits.

Wait, but for the no-hints, vision-model case: probe [1] text-only, then call 2 = pages [2..20] text-only, wait — no: with my new logic, renderSet = {1,2} (first two wanted, in range). Page 1 was already extracted by the probe (text, no render) → renderMissing = hmm, page 1 has text but no render; do I re-extract page 1 for its render? That would be a 3rd call. Ugh.

Simplify: probe with images when includeImages is true AND page 1 is a render candidate (i.e., requested[0] === 1); otherwise probe text-only and let page 1 be re-extracted in the render call. That's getting complicated.

Alternative cleaner structure that avoids proliferation: do the extraction in a single phase once `wanted` is known... but `wanted` needs totalPages which needs the probe.

Alternative: make the probe smarter — call the worker with pages `[1]` and images only if `renderSet` might include 1. Hmm.

Let me step back and consider a different, simpler implementation of the same defense: keep the current single-call-for-missing-pages logic, but when includeImages is true, pass only the *render candidates* as `missing` pages... no wait, the flag is global.

Alternative: what if, when includeImages is true, I *always* cap the worker request to the render candidates and additionally extract the remaining wanted pages in a separate text-only call? Concretely:

